    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A7095                REVISED 3/10/10\n\nTITLE  OF BILL:   An act to amend the real property tax law, in relation\nto the eligibility of persons residing in property owned  by  a  limited\npartnership to seek small claims assessment review\n\nPURPOSE  OR  GENERAL  IDEA OF BILL: The purpose of this bill is to allow\nmunicipal corporations limit the maximum allowable assessment  reduction\nin Small Claims Assessment Review (SCAR) proceedings for properties that\nare worth over one million dollars.\n\nSUMMARY  OF  SPECIFIC  PROVISIONS:   Section one amends paragraph (c) of\nsubdivision 1 of section 730 of the  Real  Property  Tax  Law  to  allow\nmunicipal  corporations  to pass a local law that would limit the amount\nby which an assessment could be reduced in SCAR proceedings for  proper-\nties  worth  over  one million dollars. The limitations prescribed would\nbe:\n\na. 25% of the threshold assessed value, plus\n\nb. Five percent of the amount by which  the  challenged  assessed  value\nexceeds the threshold assessed value.\n\nFor  the  purposes  of this section \"threshold assessed value\" means the\nassessed value that  equates  to  an  equalized  value  of  one  million\ndollars.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:    The  initial  intent of Small Claims Assessment Review\n(SCAR) was to provide to homeowners seeking small  refunds  an  informal\nand  inexpensive  venue for tax grievances. SCAR provides an alternative\nto Supreme Court, mitigating  the  need  for  legal  representation  and\nadherence  to  higher  standards  of disclosure and evidence. Due to the\ninformal nature of SCAR and its intent to  provide  modest  refunds,  it\noriginally  had  a  maximum  tax  refund  limit of $750. This tax dollar\nrefund limitation was later eliminated and replaced by the current limi-\ntation of 25% of  the  assessed  valuation  for  properties  worth  over\n$450,000.\n\nHigh-end  properties, while still residential, often are more complex to\nassess than a typical one, two or three-family residence. The complexity\nof their valuation and the minimum supporting  evidence  required  in  a\nSCAR  proceeding,  make it difficult to properly adjudicate such claims.\nIn some cases the maximum reduction allowed, 25% of the assessed  value,\nis  sought  and  granted  without  sufficient  justification - some with\ncorresponding tax reductions as high as $50,000. Because SCAR  does  not\nprovide  the  opportunity for a municipality to appeal a ruling, munici-\npalities are at a disadvantage when it comes to defending their assessed\nvalues, even when high refunds are at stake.\n\nThis  bill  would  provide  an  additional  limitation  on  the  maximum\nreductions  allowed  in  SCAR  proceedings  for  properties worth over a\nmillion dollars. In doing so, Small Claims Assessment Review  would,  as\n\noriginally  intended,  provide homeowners seeking modest refunds an easy\nand expeditious grievance venue. Large sum  reductions  would  be  arbi-\ntrated  in tax certiorari proceedings. Homeowners of high-end properties\ncould  still utilize SCAR; they simply would be limited in the amount of\na reduction they could receive.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: This is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None to State\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately\n\n",
